What each case of the decl set is for, where its name does not say it all:
the comments its declaration carried while declarations were ZON, which
JSON has no room for. tests/golden.nix describes the set.

a-nul-in-a-command-word
    A NUL, which JSON's \u0000 writes and a Nix string cannot hold: argv
    would end the word at it.

a-nul-in-a-path
    A path ends at its first NUL: /srv/a\u0000/b would be /srv/a.

a-poststop-list-at-the-records-bound
    postStop's two commands, each after the program it is run through, joined as the record keeps them: 4095 bytes, the most a record's value holds.

a-poststop-list-past-the-records-bound
    The same, one byte longer: 4096 bytes, which record.create would refuse at launch.

a-poststop-program-with-a-unit-separator
    0x1F in the program each postStop command is run through, the record's separator between two words.

a-poststop-word-with-a-newline
    A newline in a postStop word, which the session's record, lines of text, cannot hold: record.create would refuse it at launch.

a-poststop-word-with-a-record-separator
    0x1E in a postStop word, the record's separator between two commands.

a-snippet-guard
    A snippet, as the options took before commands.

an-environment-the-launch-expands
    What module.nix computes from a container's /etc/set-environment:
    references to the six the launch fills in, and PATH, which replaces the
    launch's own. And a $ spelt $$, which module.nix never writes (no value
    it computes holds a $ of its own) but a declaration written by hand may.

an-exec-in-place-of-command
    The payload printed at launch: no command.

an-unknown-field
    A Nix name translated, which no compatibility layer allows.

full
    Every field set, none to its default, as module.nix would render a busy declaration.

minimal
    Only what has no default, and a command, since a payload needs one of
    command and exec.

paths-clean-*
    The path as a protect entry, which is judged by spec.unclean.
  paths-clean-01: clean  yes  same  /a
  paths-clean-02: clean  yes  same  /a/b
  paths-clean-03: clean  yes  same  /home/u/.cache/flong
  paths-clean-04: clean  yes  same  /...
  paths-clean-05: clean  yes  same  /a b/c
  paths-clean-06: clean  yes  same  /.a/b.
  paths-clean-07: clean  yes  same  /aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa... (256 bytes)
  paths-clean-08: clean  no  same  /aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa... (257 bytes)
  paths-clean-09: clean  yes  same  /x/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa... (260 bytes)
  paths-clean-10: clean  no  same  /
  paths-clean-11: clean  no  same
  paths-clean-12: clean  no  same  a
  paths-clean-13: clean  no  same  a/b
  paths-clean-14: clean  no  same  //a
  paths-clean-15: clean  no  same  /a//b
  paths-clean-16: clean  no  same  /a/
  paths-clean-17: clean  no  same  /.
  paths-clean-18: clean  no  same  /..
  paths-clean-19: clean  no  same  /a/.
  paths-clean-20: clean  no  same  /a/./b
  paths-clean-21: clean  no  same  /a/../b
  paths-clean-22: clean  no  same  /a/..
  paths-clean-23: clean  no  differs  /aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa... (4096 bytes)
  paths-clean-24: clean  yes  same  /aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa... (4095 bytes)

paths-overlaps-*
    The first path as a bind's source, the second as a protect entry: they
    overlap when the source is refused as one that reaches it. flong check
    is lexical, as module.nix was: it compares module.nix's norm of each
    by module.nix's overlaps, so it says what the MIRROR column says the
    module did. A source that is /, /run, /run/user or flong's or the user
    manager's own under /run/user/UID is refused whatever the protect
    entry, and a path that is not clean is refused as that.
  paths-overlaps-01: overlaps  yes  same  /a  /a
  paths-overlaps-02: overlaps  yes  same  /a  /a/b
  paths-overlaps-03: overlaps  yes  same  /a/b  /a
  paths-overlaps-04: overlaps  no  same  /a  /ab
  paths-overlaps-05: overlaps  no  same  /ab  /a
  paths-overlaps-06: overlaps  no  same  /a/b  /a/c
  paths-overlaps-07: overlaps  no  same  /run/user/1000  /run/user/100
  paths-overlaps-08: overlaps  yes  same  /run/user/1000/flong  /run/user/1000
  paths-overlaps-09: overlaps  yes  differs  /  /a
  paths-overlaps-10: overlaps  yes  differs  /a  /
  paths-overlaps-11: overlaps  yes  same  /  /
  paths-overlaps-12: overlaps  no  differs  //a  /a
  paths-overlaps-13: overlaps  no  differs  /a/  /a/b
  paths-overlaps-14: overlaps  no  differs  /var/run/user/1000  /run/user/1000
  paths-overlaps-15: overlaps  no  differs  /var/run  /run/user

paths-twice-*
    Both as masks: the same destination twice is refused.
  paths-twice-01: twice  yes  same  /srv/work  /srv/work
  paths-twice-02: twice  no  same  /srv/work  /srv/work/x
  paths-twice-03: twice  no  same  /srv/work  /srv/wor
  paths-twice-04: twice  no  same  /a  /b
  paths-twice-05: twice  no  same  /a b  /a
